          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Pierre\",\"addressee\":\"Hélène\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Je vous aime!\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The expression directly presents Pierre's love for Hélène as true. Its avowal mode remains clear even though the narration and Pierre's later reflections strongly question its sincerity.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Je vous aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"later_1\",\"quotation\":\"когда он сказал эти невыходившие из него слова: «Je vous aime»\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"In this occurrence, the avowal also substantially affirms Pierre's socially established position as Hélène's fiancé and prospective husband. He says the conventional words he understands to be required in precisely this engagement situation, after accepting the blessing and silently holding his fiancée's hand. Thus the expression helps ratify the marital bond rather than merely standing beside an unrelated promise. It does not specify terms or independently create the engagement, so undertaking force is substantial but not dominant.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Пьер молча держал руку своей невесты и смотрел на ее поднимающуюся и опускающуюся прекрасную грудь.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"— Je vous aime! — сказал он, вспомнив то, чтó нужно было говорить в этих случаях; но слова эти прозвучали так бедно, что ему стало стыдно за себя.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"The words are not represented as an involuntary affective discharge. Pierre deliberately recalls and supplies the expected formula, and their poor delivery embarrasses him.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"вспомнив то, чтó нужно было говорить в этих случаях; но слова эти прозвучали так бедно, что ему стало стыдно за себя.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"No additional core force is needed beyond the love avowal and its contextual affirmation of the engagement bond. The narration explicitly characterizes the words as what Pierre believes should be said in such a situation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"— Je vous aime! — сказал он, вспомнив то, чтó нужно было говорить в этих случаях; но слова эти прозвучали так бедно, что ему стало стыдно за себя.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Pierre and Hélène have been publicly treated as an engaged couple after Prince Vasily announces his joy, blesses them, and leaves them alone. Following Hélène's kiss, Pierre remembers the conventional declaration expected in such a situation and tells her, “Je vous aime!” The delivery is hesitant and embarrassingly inadequate rather than spontaneous.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The occurrence affirms Pierre's acceptance of the fiancé and future-husband role already imposed and publicly recognized around him. Hélène gives no explicit verbal response after the focal words; her preceding kiss and participation in the engagement scene provide contextual, though not post-utterance verbal, uptake. Their marriage a month and a half later shows enactment but does not by itself determine the focal expression's force. Evidence of actual affection is mixed: the scene depicts attraction and Pierre momentarily thinks he loves her, while his later retrospective account calls the declaration a lie and says he never loved her.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as Leo Tolstoy's War and Peace. The classification relies only on the supplied evidence.\",\"limitations\":\"The excerpts establish the engagement setting, later marriage, and Pierre's retrospective denial of love, but they do not provide an explicit reply from Hélène immediately after the focal utterance. Pierre's later interpretation is relevant evidence about sincerity, not conclusive proof of his exact mental state at the earlier moment.\"}"
